<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rukn al-Dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 976</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Hasan ibn Buya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rukn al-Dawla, born Hasan ibn Buya, was one of the three Buyid brothers who founded the dynasty's rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. He took Ray and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and governed the central and northern Iranian lands until his death in 976, receiving his honorific, 'pillar of the state', from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in 946. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> was the statesman Ibn al-Amid, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> later united the Buyid realm.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rukn-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Rukn al-Dawla" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 949-977 work, CNG photograph · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buwayhids_(Buyids)._Rukn_al-Dawla_Abu_%27Ali_al-Hasan._AH_338-366_(949-977)._AR_Portrait_Dirham_(33mm,_5.69_g,_12h)._Al-Muhammadiya_mint._Dated_AH_351_(AD_962-3)_Obverse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
